        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The flight of Mercury-Atlas 5 (MA-5) was the prelude to John Glenn's orbital space flight.  MA-5 launched on November 29, 1961 carrying Enos, a five and one half-year-old male chimpanzee.  The orbital flight lasted a total of 3 hours, 20 minutes and 59 seconds, and carried the spacecraft around two orbits of the earth.
<P>
The launch of MA-5, at 10:08 a.m., was delayed approximately two and one-half hours because of holds during the countdown.  The holds included, replacement of a faulty transistor in the data receiver and installation of a piece of heat insulation on the hatch cover.  When the launch finally occurred, Enos was subjected to a 6.8-G accelerative force during the booster-engine acceleration and 7.6-G force with the rush of the sustainer engine.
<P>
During the flight, Enos carried out the lever-pulling performance and psychological tests that he had trained on for the past 16-months.  Enos performed the tasks well, and received a limited number of shocks for incorrect answers.  However during the last two sessions the center-lever on the input device failed and Enos received consecutive shocks even if the correct answer was given.  Nevertheless, the frustrated chimpanzee continued to give answers until the end of the flight. 
<P>
During the end of the first orbit, tracking stations indicated a malfunction in the environmental control system and a problem with the attitude control system.  By the second orbit, problems with the environmental control system caused Enos's body temperature to climb to 100 degrees Fahrenheit.  Although the medical monitors were willing to let the flight continue, the spacecraft's erratic attitude was too risky to deal with.  Immediately before the start of the third orbit, the decision was made to return Enos to Earth.
<P>
The MA-5 capsule touched down at 1:29 p.m., about 200 miles south of Bermuda.  The recovery ship, U.S.S. Stormes, arrived 1 hour and 20 minutes after landing.  However, there was a delay of 3 hours and 20 minutes in removing Enos from the flight capsule after it was recovered.  During the delay in getting Enos out of the couch, he broke free from his restraints, damaged several sensors, and inflicted minor injury to himself. 
<P>
The MA-5 flight met all of its objectives and was a benchmark for a manned U.S. orbital space flight.  NASA concluded that an astronaut would have been able to make the necessary attitude corrections to complete the three-orbit mission successfully.  However, they were pleased with the performance of the automatic attitude control system, which worked well for over two hours.  The results of the flight showed no significant change in Enos's physiological state or psychomotor performance, and demonstrated that the demands of spaceflight would not be excessive for human astronauts.  The flight also revealed that young chimpanzees could be trained to be highly reliable subjects for space flight studies.
<END>
